<p>An Afghan asylum seeker who had previously worked with the U.S. military in Afghanistan died in Immigration and Customs Enforcement custody less than 24 hours after his arrest in Texas, according to a U.S. veteran-led advocacy group. The man, Mohommad Nazeer Paktyawal, had been living in a Dallas-area suburb with his wife and six children while his asylum case was pending. Reuters, in reports carried by multiple outlets, said federal agents arrested him outside his apartment on Friday morning. The case has drawn attention because Paktyawal had supported U.S. forces during the war in Afghanistan and later sought protection in the United States. The cited reports attribute news of his death to the advocacy group, but they do not include a confirmed cause of death or a detailed public explanation from ICE, leaving key questions unresolved as calls for accountability grow.</p><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>U.S. veteran-led advocacy group: The group highlighted Paktyawal&#x27;s past work alongside U.S. forces in Afghanistan when it publicized his death on Sunday. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/afghan-asylum-seeker-dies-ice-custody-us-advocacy-group-says'>Reuters via Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Reuters reporting carried by multiple outlets: The reports frame the death as a custody case with major unanswered questions, noting that the cited stories do not include a confirmed cause of death or a detailed public account from ICE. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3346691/afghan-asylum-seeker-dies-ice-custody-us-advocacy-group-says'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/afghan-asylum-seeker-dies-ice-custody-us-advocacy-group-says'>Afghan asylum-seeker dies in ICE custody, US advocacy group says</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://japantoday.com/category/world/afghan-asylum-seeker-dies-in-ice-custody-us-advocacy-group-says2'>Afgha